window.COURSE = {"sections":[{"id":"overview","label":"Overview","title":"IGCSE English Refresher — 8-Week Bridge Course","group":"Start","groupEmoji":"🏁","emoji":"🚀","blurb":"What this course is, the two IGCSE routes, and the 8-week plan.","html":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Live site:</strong> https://roryquarrier.github.io/igcse-english-refresher/ — the whole course as a browsable,\nmobile-friendly web app. The source markdown lives in <code>course/</code>; the site in <code>docs/</code> is generated from it by <code>docs/build.py</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>For:</strong> a 12-year-old boy in Hong Kong (Year 8 / age 12)\n<strong>Builds towards:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE <strong>First Language English (0500)</strong> <em>or</em> <strong>English as a Second Language (0510/0511)</strong> — the two paths fork, see below.\n<strong>Time to exam:</strong> typically 3 years. This is a <strong>refresher / foundation-builder</strong>, not last-minute cramming.\n<strong>Format:</strong> 8 weeks × 4 sessions/week × ~40 min, plus one timed mock at the end.\n<strong>Total written output:</strong> ~10,000 words across short tasks and 3 extended pieces.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>1. Which IGCSE is he actually heading for?</h2>\n<p>The two English IGCSEs are <strong>separate qualifications — a school enters a candidate for one, not both.</strong> Get this right first, or half the practice is aimed at the wrong target.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>First Language English (0500/0990)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>English as a Second Language (0510/0511/0991/0993)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>For</td>\n<td>Students who use English as a <em>first</em> language</td>\n<td>Students using English alongside a strong first language (Cantonese/Mandarin)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Papers</td>\n<td><strong>Paper 1 Reading</strong> (2h, 80 marks, 50%)<br><strong>Paper 2 Directed Writing &amp; Composition</strong> (2h, 80 marks, 50%)</td>\n<td><strong>Paper 1 Reading &amp; Writing</strong><br><strong>Paper 2 Listening</strong><br><strong>Paper 3 Speaking</strong> (0511 = count-in)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skills</td>\n<td>Reading + writing only; expects native-level style and analysis</td>\n<td>Reading, writing, <strong>listening, speaking</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaking</td>\n<td>Optional Component 4, separately endorsed (not in the grade)</td>\n<td>Examined (0511) or optional (0510)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical HK schools</td>\n<td>International schools, ESF, most through-train IB schools</td>\n<td>Local / DSS schools, many EMI schools</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Which one is he doing?</strong> Ask his school. If the answer is unclear, run the <strong>Week 1 diagnostic</strong> — a lot of strong HK students sit between the two, and the diagnostic tells you which band of work to set.</p>\n<p><strong>This course covers the skills shared by both, then forks in Weeks 5–7</strong> (the directed-writing text types differ: 0500 does letter/article/speech/journal/report from a <em>reading</em> stimulus; ESL does email/report/article/review/letter from <em>bullet cues</em>).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>2. The mark schemes to aim at (0500)</h2>\n<p>If he is on the <strong>First Language</strong> route, every question he answers is buying one or more of ten Assessment Objectives. Learn them by number — they tell him exactly what a question wants.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading</strong>\n- <strong>R1</strong> — understanding of <strong>explicit</strong> meanings (what the text says)\n- <strong>R2</strong> — understanding of <strong>implicit</strong> meanings and attitudes (what it means)\n- <strong>R3</strong> — analyse, evaluate and develop facts, ideas and opinions, using support from the text\n- <strong>R4</strong> — how writers <strong>achieve effects</strong> and influence readers (word choice, imagery, sentence structure) ← most often dropped by students\n- <strong>R5</strong> — select and use information for a specific purpose (summary)</p>\n<p><strong>Writing</strong>\n- <strong>W1</strong> — articulate experience; express what is thought, felt and imagined\n- <strong>W2</strong> — organise and structure for deliberate effect (paragraphing, linking)\n- <strong>W3</strong> — range of vocabulary and sentence structures\n- <strong>W4</strong> — language appropriate to <strong>purpose</strong> and <strong>audience</strong>\n- <strong>W5</strong> — accurate <strong>spelling, punctuation, grammar</strong> ← easiest to lose, five minutes at the end protects it</p>\n<p><strong>Paper 1 (Reading), 2h, 80 marks:</strong> four questions of 20 marks on three texts (A, B, C) — comprehension, summary, a language/effects task, and an extended response. Two of the four also carry writing marks.</p>\n<p><strong>Paper 2 (Directed Writing &amp; Composition), 2h, 80 marks:</strong> Section A (40) — directed writing from a short text stimulus; Section B (40) — composition chosen from <strong>four titles (two descriptive, two narrative)</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>3. The 8-week plan</h2>\n<p>Four sessions a week. Each session = warm-up (5 min) + teaching/practice (30 min) + mini-task (5 min). Marker \"★\" = do a timed version.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Module</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n<th>Deliverable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td>01</td>\n<td><strong>Diagnostic test</strong> + set goals</td>\n<td>Scored diagnostic, target band agreed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td>02</td>\n<td><strong>Grammar &amp; punctuation foundations</strong></td>\n<td>2× error-correction sets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>03</td>\n<td><strong>Vocabulary &amp; precise expression</strong></td>\n<td>\"Replace the weak word\" set + 20-word upgrade list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td>04</td>\n<td><strong>Reading comprehension</strong> (R1/R2/R5)</td>\n<td>One 20-mark comprehension</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>05</td>\n<td><strong>Summary skills</strong> (R5)</td>\n<td>Two summaries in the exact pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6</strong></td>\n<td>06</td>\n<td><strong>Directed writing</strong> (W2/W4)</td>\n<td>One directed piece (letter/article)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7</strong></td>\n<td>07</td>\n<td><strong>Composition</strong> — descriptive + narrative (W1/W3)</td>\n<td>★ One timed composition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>8</strong></td>\n<td>08 + 09</td>\n<td><strong>Language analysis (R4)</strong> + <strong>timed mock paper</strong></td>\n<td>★ Full mock under exam time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Weekly rhythm</strong>\n- <strong>Session 1</strong> — teach the week's skill; work the <em>worked examples</em>.\n- <strong>Session 2</strong> — practice exercises; mark together with the answer key.\n- <strong>Session 3</strong> — apply to the week's <em>mini-task</em>.\n- <strong>Session 4</strong> — review errors; re-do the two hardest questions from memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Marking shorthand used throughout this course</strong> (keep a running score sheet in <code>progress.md</code>):\n<code>/5</code> for each skill area, tick-list style, so progress is visible week to week.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>4. How to use these files</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>01-diagnostic-test.md</code></td>\n<td>Placement test + mark scheme + banding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>02-foundations-grammar-punctuation.md</code></td>\n<td>Tense, agreement, sentence types, commas, apostrophes, speech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>03-vocabulary-and-expression.md</code></td>\n<td>Word choice, synonyms, \"show don't tell\", figurative language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>04-reading-comprehension.md</code></td>\n<td>Question-type tactics + practice texts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>05-summary-skills.md</code></td>\n<td>The point-selecting method, word limits, paraphrase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>06-directed-writing.md</code></td>\n<td>Letter, article, speech, report, journal structures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>07-composition-descriptive-narrative.md</code></td>\n<td>Descriptive &amp; narrative technique + planning frames</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>08-language-analysis-r4.md</code></td>\n<td>How to write about effects (0500 route)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>09-practice-paper.md</code></td>\n<td>Timed mock, Paper 1 + Paper 2 style</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>10-answer-key.md</code></td>\n<td>Answers, model answers, band descriptors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>progress.md</code></td>\n<td>Score sheet — fill in weekly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Materials to have ready:</strong> one lined exercise book (writing), one folder (reading texts), a timer, and a dictionary/thesaurus (paper or app — <em>not</em> allowed in the exam, but useful for training).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>5. Ground rules that actually raise the grade</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Every written answer is drafted, then edited.</strong> Never hand in a first draft. Editing is where W5 marks live.</li>\n<li><strong>Read the question twice; underline the command word</strong> (<em>describe, explain, summarise, how does the writer…</em>). Most lost marks are misread questions, not weak English.</li>\n<li><strong>Quote or point to the text</strong> in every reading answer. \"It says the bridge is old\" beats \"It is old\".</li>\n<li><strong>Plan for 3 minutes before any extended writing.</strong> Five-line plan = two marks of structure.</li>\n<li><strong>Time-box by marks:</strong> roughly 1.5 minutes per mark in reading, and stick to it.</li>\n</ol>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note on the exam board:</strong> figures here are read from Cambridge International's own 0500 syllabus (2027–2029) and the 0510/0511/0974 ESL specifications. Always check the current syllabus PDF for the series he will actually sit, because Paper 1 was restructured for 2027.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"id":"module-01","label":"Diagnostic Test","title":"01 — Diagnostic Test","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"🩺","blurb":"A 45-minute placement test that finds your level and your top errors.","html":"<p><strong>Time:</strong> 45 minutes (or split across two sessions). <strong>No dictionary.</strong>\n<strong>Purpose:</strong> find the band he should work at, and expose the 3–4 specific weaknesses to attack first.\n<strong>Mark it with <code>10-answer-key.md</code> and record results in <code>progress.md</code>.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Section A — Grammar &amp; accuracy (20 marks)</h2>\n<p><strong>A1. Choose the correct word. (1 mark each)</strong>\n1. The team ( has / have ) been training every morning since Monday.\n2. Neither of the boys ( was / were ) late for practice.\n3. She is the girl ( who / which ) scored in the final minute.\n4. He ran ( quick / quickly ) down the steps.\n5. If I ( was / were ) taller, I would play centre.</p>\n<p><strong>A2. Punctuate this passage. Add capitals, commas, full stops, question marks and speech marks. (8 marks)</strong></p>\n<pre><code>where are you going shouted mum from the kitchen\nto the park said ah ming\nin this rain she asked\nits not raining he lied\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>A3. Rewrite each sentence in the tense shown in brackets. (1 mark each)</strong>\n1. He eats three bowls of noodles. (past simple)\n2. They play football. (future: going to)\n3. She wrote the letter. (present perfect)\n4. I am reading a comic. (past continuous)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Section B — Vocabulary &amp; expression (10 marks)</h2>\n<p><strong>B1. Replace the blue word with a more precise word. (1 mark each)</strong>\n1. The food at the night market was very <strong>nice</strong>.\n2. He was <strong>very happy</strong> when he won the match.\n3. The walk up Tai Mo Shan was <strong>very tiring</strong>.\n4. The MTR was <strong>very crowded</strong> this morning.\n5. The horror film was <strong>very scary</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>B2. Complete the simile or metaphor. (1 mark each)</strong>\n1. The traffic was as slow as ________________.\n2. Her voice was a ________________ , cutting through the noise.\n3. The dumplings were ________________ of the steamer. <em>(metaphor)</em>\n4. The sun <em>_______________ the harb</em>ur. <em>(strong verb, not \"was\")</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Section C — Reading comprehension (20 marks)</h2>\n<p>Read the passage, then answer.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Text A</strong></p>\n<p>The old tram rattles up the hill as if it has all the time in the world. Tourists raise their phones; commuters don't look up. Inside, a boy of about twelve presses his forehead against the warm glass and watches the city slide away beneath him. Below, the harbour is a sheet of beaten metal. He has lived here all his life and he has never once grown tired of this climb. At the top, he will buy a custard tart, eat half of it, and save the rest for his grandmother, who cannot manage the hill any more. That, more than the view, is why he rides.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>C1.</strong> Where is the boy? Use a phrase from the text. <strong>(2)</strong>\n<strong>C2.</strong> What impression does the writer give of the tourists compared with the commuters? <strong>(3)</strong>\n<strong>C3.</strong> The harbour is described as \"a sheet of beaten metal\". What does this suggest about how it looks? <strong>(3)</strong>\n<strong>C4.</strong> Explain in your own words why the boy makes this journey. <strong>(4)</strong>\n<strong>C5.</strong> <em>\"as if it has all the time in the world\"</em> — what does this tell us about the tram? <strong>(3)</strong>\n<strong>C6.</strong> Find one detail that shows the boy goes up the hill often. Explain how it helps you know this. <strong>(5)</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Section D — Writing (free response, 20 marks)</h2>\n<p><strong>Choose ONE. Write 150–200 words.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Describe</em> your favourite place in Hong Kong, so that a reader who has never been there can picture it.</li>\n<li><em>Narrate</em> a story that ends with the words: \"…and that was the last time I ever took a shortcut.\"</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Assessed on:</strong> W1 (content/ideas), W2 (structure), W3 (vocabulary &amp; sentences), W4 (fit to purpose), W5 (accuracy). See the band descriptors in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Banding from the total (out of 70)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Total</th>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Start at</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–28</td>\n<td><strong>Foundation</strong></td>\n<td>Week 2 module 02 from the top; re-do A-sections each week</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29–45</td>\n<td><strong>Developing</strong></td>\n<td>Follow the 8-week plan as written</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>46–58</td>\n<td><strong>Solid</strong></td>\n<td>Compress Weeks 2–3 into one; spend the time on 08 (R4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>59–70</td>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Start at Week 4; add weekly past-paper questions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Also write down (from Section A only):</strong> the <em>top three</em> error types. Those drive the warm-ups for Weeks 2–3.</p>"},{"id":"module-02","label":"Grammar & Punctuation","title":"02 — Foundations: Grammar & Punctuation","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"✏️","blurb":"Tense, agreement, speech marks and commas - the marks you must not lose.","html":"<p><em>Weeks 2–3. Grammar is not a separate exam — it is the W5 marks on every writing question, plus the difference between a clear reading answer and a muddled one.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<p>By the end he can, without prompting:\n- keep <strong>tense consistent</strong> inside a paragraph;\n- match <strong>subject and verb</strong> (including tricky collective nouns);\n- punctuate <strong>direct speech</strong> correctly;\n- use <strong>commas</strong> for lists, clauses and clarity;\n- choose the right <strong>sentence type</strong> for effect (simple / compound / complex).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 1 — Tense consistency</h2>\n<p><strong>Key idea:</strong> a paragraph usually lives in <em>one</em> tense. Switch only when the <em>time</em> changes — and switch deliberately.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Past simple</strong> — finished actions: <em>He scored.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Past continuous</strong> — background action: <em>He was running when he scored.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Present perfect</strong> — past action with a link to now: <em>He has scored three goals this season.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong> — <em>He will score / He is going to score.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Worked example (fix the tense):</strong>\n- ✗ <em>He walks into the room and saw the trophy on the table.</em>\n- ✓ <em>He walked into the room and saw the trophy on the table.</em> (both past)</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 1</strong> — underline any verb that breaks the tense of its sentence, then correct it.\n1. She opens the door, looks around, and screamed.\n2. Yesterday we are going to the beach.\n3. He has finished his homework an hour ago.\n4. The MTR was crowded, so we decide to walk.</p>\n<p><em>Answers: 1. screamed→screams (or opens→opened…); 2. are going→went; 3. has finished→finished; 4. decide→decided.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 2 — Subject–verb agreement</h2>\n<p><strong>Key idea:</strong> the verb agrees with the <strong>subject</strong>, not with the nearest noun.\n- <em>The box of old photos <strong>is</strong> in the loft.</em> (subject = box)\n- <em>Each of the players <strong>has</strong> a locker.</em> (each = singular)\n- <em>Neither of the boys <strong>was</strong> late.</em> (neither = singular)\n- Collective nouns (<em>team, family, class</em>) take <strong>singular</strong> in British usage: <em>The team <strong>is</strong> ready.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Practice 2</strong> — choose the correct form.\n1. The list of names (was/were) long.\n2. Neither the player nor the coach (was/were) happy.\n3. There (is/are) three reasons for this.\n4. The news (is/are) good.</p>\n<p><em>Answers: 1. was 2. was 3. are (inverted — subject is \"three reasons\") 4. is.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 3 — Punctuating direct speech</h2>\n<p><strong>Rules:</strong>\n- Speech goes in <strong>\" \"</strong> (double) or <strong>' '</strong> (single) — be consistent.\n- The <strong>comma or question mark sits inside</strong> the closing speech mark: <em>\"Where are you going?\" she asked.</em>\n- The <strong>speaker's name stays lowercase</strong> if the reporting clause continues the same sentence: <em>\"Wait,\" he said, \"I'm coming.\"</em>\n- <strong>New speaker = new line.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&quot;Give me one more minute,&quot; pleaded Sam.\n&quot;Not a second longer,&quot; replied the coach.\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Practice 3</strong> — punctuate (8 marks):</p>\n<pre><code>where have you been asked his sister\nat the arcade he answered quietly\nyou were supposed to be home an hour ago she said\ni know he said but i lost track of time\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>Model answer in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 4 — Sentence types for effect</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Shape</th>\n<th>Use it to…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simple</strong></td>\n<td>one clause</td>\n<td>slow the pace, hit hard: <em>He stopped.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compound</strong></td>\n<td>two clauses + and/but/or/so</td>\n<td>balance ideas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Complex</strong></td>\n<td>main + subordinate (because, although, when, if)</td>\n<td>show cause, contrast, time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Minor/verbless</strong></td>\n<td>no verb</td>\n<td>drama: <em>Silence. Then footsteps.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a long, comma-spliced sentence dulls the reader. A short sentence after a long one <strong>pops</strong>. Vary deliberately.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 4</strong> — rewrite this dull passage using three <em>different</em> sentence types to build tension:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>He walked down the dark corridor. He heard a sound. He turned around. There was nothing there.\n<em>See <code>10-answer-key.md</code> for one strong model.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 5 — Commas, apostrophes, semicolons</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comma</strong> — lists: <em>I bought apples, oranges, and pears.</em> After an opening clause: <em>After the match, we ate.</em> Marking extra info: <em>My cousin, who lives in Macau, is visiting.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Apostrophe</strong> — possession (<em>the player's boots; the players' boots</em>) and contraction (<em>it's = it is; its = belonging to it</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Semicolon</strong> — joins two closely related clauses: <em>It was late; we went home.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Top error in HK scripts:</strong> <em>its / it's</em> and <em>your / you're</em>. Drill these.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Practice 5</strong> — insert apostrophes where needed; fix <em>its/it's</em>.\n1. The boy's and girls teams both won. <em>(careful — where does the apostrophe go?)</em>\n2. Its raining, and the trams in the city are its slowest when wet.\n3. The dogs bones were buried in the neighbours garden.\n<em>Answers: 1. boys' and girls' (apostrophe after each plural noun); 2. It's raining … the city's trams are their slowest when wet (fix: Its→It's, its→the city's/their); 3. dogs' bones … the neighbour's garden.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Week 2–3 mini-tasks</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Edit a passage:</strong> take one paragraph of your own writing and mark every error using a coloured pen, then rewrite it cleanly. <em>(2× per week)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Error log:</strong> start a page titled \"My Top 5 Errors.\" Add one example each time you make a mistake. Re-read before every piece of writing.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Grammar /5</code> — all verbs agree and stay in tense; <code>Punctuation /5</code> — speech + commas + apostrophes correct.</p>"},{"id":"module-03","label":"Vocabulary","title":"03 — Vocabulary & Precise Expression","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"💬","blurb":"Swap weak words for precise ones and make your writing come alive.","html":"<p><em>Week 3. The difference between a middle-band and a top-band script is rarely grammar — it is <strong>word choice</strong>. \"Very nice\" costs marks; \"sizzling\" earns them.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Replace <strong>weak, vague words</strong> (\"nice\", \"big\", \"very + adjective\") with precise ones.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>figurative language</strong> (simile, metaphor, personification) <em>sparingly and accurately</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Show, don't tell</strong> — imply emotion through detail.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 1 — Kill the weak words</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Weak</th>\n<th>Stronger options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>nice (food)</td>\n<td>delicious, fragrant, savoury, moreish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>big</td>\n<td>enormous, towering, vast, colossal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>very tired</td>\n<td>exhausted, shattered, drained</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>very hot</td>\n<td>scorching, sweltering, blistering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>walked slowly</td>\n<td>shuffled, trudged, ambled, drifted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>said</td>\n<td>muttered, snapped, whispered, insisted</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> if you've written <strong>\"very + adjective\"</strong>, find the one word that already means it. <em>Very hungry → famished. Very cold → freezing.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Practice 1</strong> — replace the bold word with a precise one (no repeats):\n1. The queue for the Star Ferry was <strong>very long</strong>.\n2. He was <strong>very scared</strong> during the typhoon.\n3. The roast goose smelled <strong>nice</strong>.\n4. She <strong>walked</strong> proudly onto the stage.\n5. The exam was <strong>very hard</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Sample answers in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 2 — Figurative language</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simile</strong> — <em>like / as</em>: <em>Her laugh was like a door slamming.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Metaphor</strong> — says it <em>is</em>: <em>The city was a furnace.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Personification</strong> — gives human qualities to things: <em>The wind clawed at the windows.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Pathetic fallacy</strong> — weather mirroring mood: dark clouds before bad news.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Warning:</strong> one strong image beats five weak ones. Clichés (<em>as busy as a bee</em>, <em>raining cats and dogs</em>) score <strong>nothing</strong> — examiners have read them a thousand times.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 2</strong> — turn each dull sentence into one with a single strong image.\n1. It was raining hard.\n2. He was nervous before the interview.\n3. The classroom was noisy.\n4. It was a happy day.\n<em>Models in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 3 — Show, don't tell</h2>\n<p><strong>Telling:</strong> <em>He was embarrassed.</em>\n<strong>Showing:</strong> <em>A red flush crept up his neck; he studied his shoes as if they held the answers.</em></p>\n<p>Showing works by giving the reader <strong>evidence</strong> (a gesture, a detail, a sensation) and letting them name the emotion.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 3</strong> — convert:\n1. She was excited. → (a gesture + a detail)\n2. The old man was lonely. → (an object + an action)\n3. I was angry. → (a physical sensation + a short sentence)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 4 — Building a personal word bank</h2>\n<p>For each topic he writes about, collect an <strong>8-word upgrade list</strong>. Over 8 weeks, that is ~60 precise words he owns.</p>\n<p><strong>Starter bank — Hong Kong scenes</strong>\n<em>weather:</em> humid, muggy, oppressive, drizzle, squall\n<em>crowds:</em> jostling, bottleneck, throng, shoulder-to-shoulder\n<em>food:</em> fragrant, glistening, crisp, chewy, steaming\n<em>city:</em> neon, maze, fogbound, razor-thin, vertiginous (spire of towers)\n<em>emotion:</em> dread, elation, resentment, relief, awe</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 4</strong> — write a <strong>60-word paragraph</strong> describing a Hong Kong street using <strong>at least five</strong> words from the bank above. Underline them.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Mini-task (this week)</h2>\n<p>Take a piece of your Week 1 writing. Re-write it with <strong>no sentence containing \"very\"</strong> and <strong>at least three precise verbs</strong>. Compare the two versions side by side.</p>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Word choice /5</code> — no vague fillers; <code>Imagery /5</code> — images fresh and accurate, not clichés.</p>"},{"id":"module-04","label":"Reading","title":"04 — Reading Comprehension","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"📖","blurb":"Answer the question that was actually asked, and back every point with the text.","html":"<p><em>Week 4. Covers R1 (explicit), R2 (implicit/attitudes), R5 (select for a purpose). R4 (effects) gets its own module — 08.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Identify the <strong>command word</strong> and the <strong>number of marks</strong> before writing.</li>\n<li>Answer <strong>explicit</strong> questions exactly (R1) using the text's own detail.</li>\n<li>Read <strong>implicit</strong> meaning — attitude, tone, implication (R2).</li>\n<li>Support <strong>every</strong> point with a <strong>quotation or reference</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 1 — De-code the question first</h2>\n<p>Underline the command word. It tells you the answer's shape:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>What they want</th>\n<th>Shape of answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Give / State</em></td>\n<td>one fact</td>\n<td>a phrase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Explain</em></td>\n<td>a reason, in your own words</td>\n<td>because + point</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Describe</em></td>\n<td>detail from the text</td>\n<td>2–3 references</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>How does the writer…</em></td>\n<td>analysis of effect (R4)</td>\n<td>quote → effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Summarise</em></td>\n<td>selected points (R5)</td>\n<td>bullet-proof prose, word-limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>What impression…</em></td>\n<td>attitude/vibe (R2)</td>\n<td>adjective + evidence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Marks = points.</strong> A 3-mark question wants <strong>three</strong> separate ideas, each with support. Never write one good sentence and stop.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 2 — R1: explicit answers</h2>\n<p>Copy the <em>precise</em> detail — don't approximate. If the text says \"about twelve\", \"around ten\" loses the mark.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 1</strong> — from Text A (Week 1 diagnostic):\nQ: Where is the boy? (2)\nModel: <em>The boy is on an old tram that is climbing up the hill (1); he is inside it, pressing his forehead against the warm glass (1).</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 3 — R2: implicit meaning and attitude</h2>\n<p>Watch the <strong>signals</strong>: <em>although, however, despite, unfortunately, but, even so</em>. These flip or colour the meaning. An attitude question usually needs an <strong>adjective + evidence</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice 2</strong> — from Text A: <em>\"commuters don't look up.\"</em> What impression does this give? (3)\nModel: <em>It shows the journey is ordinary for locals (1); they are so used to the route that they ignore it (1); only the tourists, \"raising their phones\", find it worth attention (1).</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Lesson 4 — R5/main idea: pull it together</h2>\n<p>For \"what is the text mainly about\" style questions, name the <strong>subject</strong> and the writer's <strong>angle on it</strong>, not just the subject.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Practice text — \"The Cheung Chau Bun Scramble\"</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Every year, on an island an hour's ferry ride from the city, a tower of buns rises in a village square. At midnight a whistle blows and dozens of climbers throw themselves at it, hauling themselves up the bamboo scaffolding while the crowd roars. They are not paid. They are not famous. For most of the year they are students, or chefs, or delivery riders. But for those three minutes, on one night in May, they are heroes of an island that knows every one of their names.</p>\n<p>The tradition nearly vanished. In 1978 the tower collapsed in a heap of bamboo and screaming men; forty people were hurt and the race was banned for years. When it returned, it came back small, careful, and wrapped in safety nets — and somehow, the village decided, even better. Not because danger is gone, but because the risk is now shared and understood rather than hidden.</p>\n<p>The buns themselves are not eaten. They are blessed, hauled down, and given away, which is, when you think about it, the whole point.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Questions (20 marks total):</strong>\n1. What happens at midnight on the island? (2)\n2. Using the passage, describe who the climbers are. (3)\n3. What impression does the writer give of the climbers' everyday lives? (3)\n4. Explain why the race was stopped in 1978. (3)\n5. The writer says the race came back \"even better\". Explain what he means. (4)\n6. Find one detail that suggests the island community is close-knit, and explain how it shows this. (5)</p>\n<p><em>Model answers in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Mini-task</h2>\n<p>Write <strong>your own</strong> 6-mark question on the Cheung Chau passage. Then write the model answer a top candidate would give. <em>(Setting questions is the fastest way to learn what questions want.)</em></p>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Reading /5</code> — every point supported by the text; <code>Implicit /5</code> — attitude questions answered with an adjective + evidence.</p>"},{"id":"module-05","label":"Summary Skills","title":"05 — Summary Skills (R5)","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"📝","blurb":"The most teachable question on the paper - there is a method, and it wins marks.","html":"<p><em>Week 5. Summary is the most <strong>teachable</strong> question on the paper: there is a method, and the method wins marks.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Select <strong>only</strong> the relevant points for the stated focus.</li>\n<li><strong>Paraphrase</strong> rather than copy.</li>\n<li>Write in <strong>continuous prose</strong> within the <strong>word limit</strong>.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>linking words</strong> to create flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>The method (follow it every time)</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read the focus line twice.</strong> E.g. <em>\"Summarise the <strong>benefits</strong> of the bridge and the <strong>problems</strong> it caused.\"</em> — two lists, not one.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-read the text with a pencil.</strong> Every time you meet a relevant point, number it in the margin. Ignore examples, jokes, and repetition.</li>\n<li><strong>List your points as bullets</strong> — one line each, in your own words.</li>\n<li><strong>Group and order</strong> them (benefits together, problems together).</li>\n<li><strong>Write continuous prose</strong> using connectives: <em>also, in addition, furthermore, however, on the other hand, as a result</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Count words.</strong> Stay at or under the limit. Never exceed it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Traps:</strong>\n- <strong>Copying whole phrases</strong> — quotes do not count for R5. Paraphrase.\n- <strong>Adding your own opinion</strong> — a summary has no opinion. Ever.\n- <strong>Writing one long unbroken block</strong> — group by theme.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Worked example</h2>\n<p>Focus: <em>\"Summarise the problems caused by the new bridge.\"</em> (limit ~70 words)</p>\n<p><strong>Text extract:</strong> <em>Within months, the bridge drew thousands of visitors. Residents complained that the village had become a car park. The narrow lanes, once quiet, filled with idling coaches. Bakers who had thrived on local trade found tourists bought nothing and left litter. Some families moved away; others fought the council for a barrier that never came.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Point list:</strong> traffic congestion → streets used as parking → tour buses idle in lanes → local shops lost regular trade → litter → families leaving → no help from council.</p>\n<p><strong>Model summary (63 words):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>The bridge brought serious problems. Traffic congestion increased, with village streets acting as car parks and coaches idling in the once-quiet lanes. Local businesses, especially bakers, lost regular customers because tourists spent little and dropped litter. Consequently some families left, while others were left disappointed when the council failed to provide any barrier.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Notice: <strong>no opinion</strong>, <strong>linked</strong>, <strong>under the limit</strong>, and every point <strong>paraphrased</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Practice (Week 5)</h2>\n<p><strong>Text: \"The Escalator\"</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The Central–Mid-Levels escalator was built to solve a simple problem: commuting up a steep hill. For residents it became a lifeline — no more taxis, no more sweat. For shop owners below the first stop, it was ruin: foot traffic flowed overhead and never touched their doorsteps. For the city, it became a curiosity that tourists queued to ride, photographing laundry lines and neon from above. And for the young couple who opened a noodle shop halfway up, whose rent the escalator had made suddenly affordable, it was the luckiest accident of their lives.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Task:</strong> Summarise the <strong>different effects</strong> the escalator had on <strong>three</strong> groups of people. <strong>(8 marks, limit 80 words.)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Model answer in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Mini-task</h2>\n<p>Take any news article this week. Write a <strong>50-word summary</strong> with the focus: <em>\"Summarise the main argument, ignoring examples.\"</em> Then count your words — no marks for going over.</p>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Selection /5</code> — only relevant points, none missed; <code>Paraphrase /5</code> — own words, linked, within limit.</p>"},{"id":"module-06","label":"Directed Writing","title":"06 — Directed Writing","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"✉️","blurb":"Letters, articles, speeches and reports - matching your words to your reader.","html":"<p><em>Week 6. W2 (structure) + W4 (register/purpose). Covers both IGCSE routes, then a note on which to use.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Match <strong>register</strong> to <strong>audience</strong> (friend vs headteacher vs newspaper reader).</li>\n<li>Use a <strong>clear structure</strong> for each text type.</li>\n<li>Cover <strong>every bullet/instruction</strong> — nothing missed.</li>\n<li>Open and close <strong>appropriately</strong> for the form.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Part 1 — Register: who is reading this?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Audience</th>\n<th>Register</th>\n<th>Open with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A friend</td>\n<td>informal, contractions, humour</td>\n<td><em>Hi Jay — guess what happened.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A teacher / headteacher</td>\n<td>formal, polite, no contractions</td>\n<td><em>Dear Mrs Chan,</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A newspaper</td>\n<td>lively, opinionated, some rhetorical questions</td>\n<td><em>Hook in the first line</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown official</td>\n<td><em>Dear Sir/Madam</em> → <em>Yours faithfully</em></td>\n<td>formal throughout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Named person</td>\n<td><em>Dear Mr Lee</em> → <em>Yours sincerely</em></td>\n<td>formal but personal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> never mix. <em>\"Dear Sir/Madam, how's it going?\"</em> fails W4 instantly.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Part 2 — Structures for each form</h2>\n<h3>Informal letter / email (ESL &amp; 0500)</h3>\n<pre><code>Hi [Name],\nOpening — friendly line + purpose.\nPara 1 — first bullet/point, developed with a detail or example.\nPara 2 — second point, developed (add an opinion: I think / it'd be great if…).\nPara 3 — third point.\nClosing — warm sign-off: Take care / Write back soon,\n[Your name]\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Develop every point</strong> — one thin sentence per bullet loses marks. Add <strong>detail, example, or opinion</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Formal letter (complaint / request)</h3>\n<pre><code>Dear [Name or Sir/Madam],\nPara 1 — purpose clearly: &quot;I am writing to complain about…&quot;\nPara 2 — the problem + evidence.\nPara 3 — the effect it had on me/others.\nPara 4 — what I want done (a specific request).\nYours sincerely / faithfully, [Full name]\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Article (magazine / school paper)</h3>\n<pre><code>[CATCHY HEADLINE]\nHook — a question, a startling fact, or a mini-anecdote.\nBody — 2–3 paragraphs, each with a clear point + example.\nEnd — a punchy final line or call to action.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use <strong>first/second person</strong> to involve the reader: <em>\"Ever noticed how…? You probably have.\"</em></p>\n<h3>Speech / talk (0500 route often asks for this)</h3>\n<pre><code>Address the audience: &quot;Good morning, everyone.&quot;\nState your position early.\nThree clear points, each with evidence or a short story.\nAcknowledge the other side briefly — then answer it.\nEnd with a strong, memorable close (repeat your key idea).\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Journal / diary entry</h3>\n<p>First person, <strong>reflective</strong>, past tense, emotional honesty. Not a report — an <em>inner</em> voice. <em>\"I keep thinking about what he said…\"</em></p>\n<h3>Report (ESL Paper 2 Section B)</h3>\n<pre><code>[TITLE]\nIntroduction — what this report is about.\nFindings — what you discovered (sub-headings ok).\nRecommendations — what should happen next.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Factual, formal, no jokes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Part 3 — The 3-minute plan</h2>\n<p>Before writing <strong>any</strong> directed piece, jot:\n1. <strong>Form + audience</strong> (register).\n2. <strong>The three or four points</strong> you must cover (or the bullet cues).\n3. <strong>One strong opening line.</strong></p>\n<p>Then write. Then <strong>check every bullet</strong> is covered — a missing bullet is an automatic cap on the mark.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Practice (choose the route that matches his exam)</h2>\n<p><strong>0500 route:</strong> <em>\"Write a letter to the editor of your local newspaper arguing that Hong Kong should build more public sports facilities for teenagers. Use ideas from the article and your own experience.\"</em> <strong>(40 marks across two tasks.)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>ESL route:</strong> <em>Your school is organising a charity fun run. Write an email to a friend inviting them, covering these three bullet points: (1) what the event is, (2) why you think it will be fun, (3) what they need to bring.</em> <strong>(~150–200 words.)</strong></p>\n<p><em>Model answers in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Mini-task</h2>\n<p>Write the <strong>same three sentences</strong> twice: once for a <strong>friend</strong>, once for the <strong>headteacher</strong>. Highlight every difference in word choice. That contrast <em>is</em> W4.</p>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Purpose/form /5</code> — conventions of the form present; <code>Register /5</code> — consistent and matched to audience; <code>Coverage /5</code> — every bullet handled and developed.</p>"},{"id":"module-07","label":"Composition","title":"07 — Composition: Descriptive & Narrative","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"🎨","blurb":"Descriptive and narrative writing: plan it, pace it, land the ending.","html":"<p><em>Week 7. W1 (experience/expression) + W3 (vocabulary/sentences) + W2 (structure). On Paper 2 Section B he chooses from <strong>four titles: two descriptive, two narrative.</strong> Know which he's stronger at — and why.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Plan a descriptive piece using the <strong>senses + one controlling impression</strong>.</li>\n<li>Plan a narrative using a <strong>clear arc</strong> (situation → complication → turning point → resolution).</li>\n<li>Control <strong>pace</strong> with sentence length and paragraph breaks.</li>\n<li>Avoid the <strong>three killers</strong>: no plan, clichéd endings, flat characters.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Part A — Descriptive writing</h2>\n<p><strong>The controlling idea.</strong> Decide <em>one</em> impression the place/scene should leave: <em>peaceful, threatening, chaotic, nostalgic</em>. Every detail chosen should serve it. Random detail = no marks.</p>\n<p><strong>The senses frame</strong> — before writing, list:\n- <strong>What he sees</strong> (3 details, one unusual)\n- <strong>Hears</strong> (2)\n- <strong>Smells or tastes</strong> (1)\n- <strong>Feels on the skin</strong> (1)\nThen <strong>zoom in</strong> on one tiny thing and <strong>zoom out</strong> to the whole scene.</p>\n<p><strong>Move the reader's eye</strong> like a camera: wide shot → medium → close-up. Don't teleport.</p>\n<p><strong>Model opening (HK wet market):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Under the strip lights the market breathes. A hundred voices fold into one warm noise — the slap of a cleaver, the hiss of steam, a vendor's laugh bouncing off the wet floor. Somewhere at the back, where the light goes grey, a fish glints once, and is still.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Notice: sensory, an unusual verb (\"breathes\"), one controlling impression (life, warmth), and no clichés.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> plan and write <strong>one paragraph (80 words)</strong> describing a <strong>typhoon</strong> approaching, using the senses frame and a single controlling impression (<strong>dread</strong>).</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Part B — Narrative writing</h2>\n<p><strong>The arc:</strong>\n1. <strong>Situation</strong> — who, where, when. In <em>early</em>.\n2. <strong>Complication</strong> — the problem arrives. Raise a question.\n3. <strong>Turning point</strong> — the moment things change.\n4. <strong>Resolution</strong> — show the <em>change</em> in the character, not just an event.</p>\n<p><strong>Show character through action, not description.</strong> <em>He was brave</em> = telling. <em>He stepped between his sister and the dog</em> = showing.</p>\n<p><strong>Pace control:</strong>\n- <strong>Fast:</strong> short sentences, action verbs, dialogue.\n- <strong>Slow:</strong> long sentences, description, reflection.\nSwap deliberately to make the reader's heart rate move with the story.</p>\n<p><strong>Endings:</strong> avoid <em>\"…and then I woke up\"</em> and <em>\"…it was all a dream.\"</em> Instead, end on a small, earned change: a decision, an object noticed, a line of dialogue.</p>\n<p><strong>Model (growth ending):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>He didn't go back to the arcade that week, or the next. When his grandmother asked why, he only shrugged — but he left the trophy on her shelf, where she could see it, and where, every morning, she did.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> plan and write the <strong>turning point paragraph (80 words)</strong> of a story titled <em>\"The Shortcut\"</em> — using one short sentence to punch the moment.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>The 3-minute plan (both types)</h2>\n<p><strong>Descriptive:</strong> <em>Controlling impression → 6 sensory details → camera moves (wide→close).</em>\n<strong>Narrative:</strong> <em>Situation → Complication → Turning point → Resolution + the word/line you must end on.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Week 7 mini-tasks</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>★ <strong>Timed composition:</strong> 45 minutes, 350–450 words, choose one of: (a) <em>Describe a place where you feel completely safe</em>; (b) <em>Write a story that begins \"The lights went out…\"</em> Mark with the band descriptors.</li>\n<li>Rewrite your <strong>weakest paragraph</strong> from that composition — then compare. The rewrite is where the marks live.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Content/ideas /5</code> — vivid, relevant, developed; <code>Structure /5</code> — clear arc or clear camera movement; <code>Vocab/sentences /5</code> — varied and precise; <code>Accuracy /5</code> — few errors.</p>"},{"id":"module-08","label":"Language Analysis","title":"08 — Language Analysis: How Writers Create Effects (R4)","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"🔍","blurb":"How writers create effects - say what the words do to the reader.","html":"<p><em>Week 8. <strong>First Language (0500) route mainly.</strong> R4 is the objective students most often drop — and it carries part of two separate Paper 1 questions. If he is on the ESL route, this module still sharpens his reading but is not directly examined.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Learning goals</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Spot the writer's <strong>techniques</strong> (word choice, imagery, sentence structure, tone).</li>\n<li>Write about <strong>effect on the reader</strong>, not just <em>name</em> the device.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>quote → effect</strong> pattern fluently.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>The core mistake</h2>\n<p><strong>Weak (naming):</strong> <em>The writer uses a metaphor here.</em>\n<strong>Strong (effect):</strong> <em>The metaphor \"a sheet of beaten metal\" makes the harbour sound hard and motionless, so the view feels frozen and slightly cold, and we sense the boy finds it beautiful but distant.</em></p>\n<p>Naming the device earns <strong>nothing</strong>. Explaining what it makes the reader <em>feel/see/think</em> earns the mark.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>The method: Q-E-E</h2>\n<p><strong>Quote</strong> the exact words → <strong>Explain</strong> the technique/meaning → <strong>Effect</strong> on the reader (or on the mood).</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>\"the old tram rattles up the hill as if it has all the time in the world\"</em>\n<strong>Q:</strong> \"as if it has all the time in the world\"\n<strong>E:</strong> personifies the tram, giving it a leisurely human attitude, and the verb \"rattles\" suggests age and weariness.\n<strong>Effect:</strong> it makes the climb feel unhurried and timeless, so the reader slows down too — and we understand the boy's calm, familiar affection for a daily journey.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>What to look for (checklist)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Ask</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Word choice</strong></td>\n<td>Why <em>this</em> word? What connotation does it carry?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Imagery</strong> (simile/metaphor/personification)</td>\n<td>What picture? What mood?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sentence length</strong></td>\n<td>Short = tension/impact. Long = calm/flow. Why here?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Punctuation</strong></td>\n<td>Dashes, ellipses, exclamation — what feel?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sound</strong></td>\n<td>Alliteration, sibilance, repetition — what rhythm?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tone/register</strong></td>\n<td>Formal? Chatty? Ironic?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure</strong></td>\n<td>What is placed first/last? What builds up?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Practice (15 marks)</h2>\n<p>Re-read the <strong>Cheung Chau</strong> passage (module 04) and answer:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>\"a tower of buns rises in a village square\"</em> — what does the writer's word choice make you picture? <strong>(3)</strong></li>\n<li><em>\"they throw themselves at it\"</em> — what does this show about the climbers? <strong>(3)</strong></li>\n<li><em>\"came back small, careful, and wrapped in safety nets\"</em> — what is the effect of the three-part list? <strong>(4)</strong></li>\n<li><em>\"which is, when you think about it, the whole point.\"</em> — what tone do these closing words create, and why? <strong>(5)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Model answers in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Mini-task</h2>\n<p>Find <strong>three</strong> sentences in any article this week. For each, write one Q-E-E paragraph about the writer's technique. Aim for the <em>effect</em>, never the label.</p>\n<h2>Marking guide</h2>\n<p><code>Effects /5</code> — effect on reader explained, not just device named; <code>Evidence /5</code> — precise quotes; <code>Expression /5</code> — analytical phrasing (<em>\"this suggests…\", \"the reader feels…\"</em>).</p>"},{"id":"module-09","label":"Mock Paper","title":"09 — Timed Mock Paper","group":"Modules","groupEmoji":"📚","emoji":"⏱️","blurb":"A full timed paper, sat under exam conditions.","html":"<p><em>Week 8. Sit under exam conditions: no dictionary, no phone, timer visible. Two sittings recommended.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Sitting 1 — Paper 1 style: Reading (60 minutes, 40 marks)</h2>\n<h3>Text A</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Kwun Tong at seven in the morning smells of rain and bleach. The wet market traders hose down their stalls before the city wakes, and the water runs in dark ribbons between crates of cabbage and cages of pale, craning chickens. Mrs Ho has run the same stall for thirty-one years. She can tell you the price of everything and the name of almost everyone. When the estate across the road was demolished last spring, she stood in the empty stalls of her neighbours and cried — not for the buildings, she says, but for the habits. \"You know a person by how they buy fish,\" she says. \"Now I don't know anyone.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q1.</strong> Where and when is this text set? <strong>(2)</strong>\n<strong>Q2.</strong> Using the text, describe Mrs Ho. <strong>(3)</strong>\n<strong>Q3.</strong> <em>\"the water runs in dark ribbons\"</em> — what does this image suggest? <strong>(4)</strong>\n<strong>Q4.</strong> What impression does the writer give of how Mrs Ho feels about the demolition? Use evidence. <strong>(5)</strong>\n<strong>Q5.</strong> <em>\"Now I don't know anyone.\"</em> — explain the effect of this short sentence as the text's ending. <strong>(6)</strong></p>\n<h3>Text B (summary focus)</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The new community garden was built on an empty lot that had been used for parking. Within a season it changed the estate. Children who had played indoors now grew beans and argued over watering rotas. Elderly residents, many of whom had not spoken to a neighbour in years, found themselves with a reason to leave their flats. Rents on the surrounding blocks rose slightly as the area became more desirable. Some long-time tenants worried that this would price out the very people the garden had brought together.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q6.</strong> Summarise the effects of the community garden on the estate. <strong>(12 marks, limit 100 words.)</strong></p>\n<h3>Q7. Extended response</h3>\n<p><em>\"Places matter more to us for the habits they hold than for the buildings they contain.\"</em> Using ideas from <strong>both texts</strong>, write about how far you agree. <strong>(8 marks)</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Sitting 2 — Paper 2 style: Writing (60 minutes, 40 marks)</h2>\n<p><strong>Section A — Directed writing (20 marks)</strong>\nWrite a <strong>letter to the editor</strong> of a local newspaper arguing that more community spaces like the garden in Text B should be created in Hong Kong. Use ideas from the reading texts and your own experience. <em>(~250–300 words.)</em>\n- Register: formal, persuasive\n- Structure: letter conventions + 3 developed arguments + counter-argument answered</p>\n<p><strong>Section B — Composition (20 marks)</strong>\nChoose <strong>one</strong>, 350–450 words:\n1. <em>Describe</em> a place in Hong Kong that is disappearing.\n2. <em>Describe</em> a market at the moment it closes for the day.\n3. <em>Write a story that ends with the words:</em> \"…and nobody ever mentioned it again.\"\n4. <em>Write a story about a character who is given a second chance.</em></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Marking</h2>\n<p>Use the band descriptors in <code>10-answer-key.md</code>. Record both sittings in <code>progress.md</code>. <strong>Re-do the two weakest questions only</strong> — not the whole paper — after marking.</p>"},{"id":"answer-key","label":"Answer Key","title":"10 — Answer Key & Band Descriptors","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"✅","blurb":"Every answer, model answer and band descriptor.","html":"<hr />\n<h2>Diagnostic test — Section A</h2>\n<p><strong>A1 (5):</strong> 1. has  2. was  3. who  4. quickly  5. were</p>\n<p><strong>A2 — punctuated passage (8):</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&quot;Where are you going?&quot; shouted Mum from the kitchen.\n&quot;To the park,&quot; said Ah Ming.\n&quot;In this rain?&quot; she asked.\n&quot;It's not raining,&quot; he lied.\n</code></pre>\n<p><em>Award 1 each for: speech marks on all lines; capital at each sentence start; correct comma/question-mark placement; \"Mum\" capitalised; \"It's\" apostrophe; new line per speaker.</em></p>\n<p><strong>A3 (4):</strong> 1. He ate three bowls of noodles.  2. They are going to play football.  3. She has written the letter.  4. I was reading a comic.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Diagnostic test — Section B</h2>\n<p><strong>B1 (5) — any sensible precise word:</strong> 1. delicious/fragrant  2. delighted/elated  3. exhausting/gruelling  4. packed/jammed/crammed  5. terrifying/chilling</p>\n<p><strong>B2 (5) — sample accepted answers:</strong> 1. as slow as a snail in mud / as slow as the Central escalator at 9am  2. her voice was a blade (or a knife)  3. the dumplings were clouds of the steamer  4. the sun <strong>sliced/blazed across</strong> the harbour</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Diagnostic test — Section C (Text A)</h2>\n<p><strong>C1 (2):</strong> on an old tram climbing the hill / inside it, forehead against the warm glass.\n<strong>C2 (3):</strong> tourists are curious and attentive — they \"raise their phones\"; commuters are indifferent — they \"don't look up\"; the contrast shows the journey is ordinary for locals but a spectacle for visitors.\n<strong>C3 (3):</strong> \"beaten metal\" suggests a hard, flat, gleaming surface; it implies the light on the water is metallic and bright; it makes the harbour sound almost still and cold rather than welcoming.\n<strong>C4 (4):</strong> he climbs to buy a custard tart; he eats half and saves the rest for his grandmother; she can no longer manage the hill; so he makes the trip for her, not for the view.\n<strong>C5 (3):</strong> it gives the tram a human, unhurried quality; the climb feels slow and timeless; it reflects the boy's calm, patient relationship with the journey.\n<strong>C6 (5):</strong> e.g. \"he has lived here all his life\" — shows long familiarity; \"he has never once grown tired of this climb\" — shows it is a repeated, habitual journey; <em>or</em> \"At the top, he will buy…\" implying a routine. Must explain <strong>how</strong> the detail proves frequency.</p>\n<p><strong>Section D (20):</strong> see band descriptors below. Award nothing above Developing if there is <strong>no plan</strong> visible or the response is under 120 words.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 02 answers</h2>\n<p><strong>P1:</strong> 1. opens→opened (or screamed→screams)  2. are going→went  3. has finished→finished  4. decide→decided\n<strong>P2:</strong> 1. was  2. was  3. are  4. is\n<strong>P3 model:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&quot;Where have you been?&quot; asked his sister.\n&quot;At the arcade,&quot; he answered quietly.\n&quot;You were supposed to be home an hour ago,&quot; she said.\n&quot;I know,&quot; he said, &quot;but I lost track of time.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>P4 model (varied sentence types):</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>He walked down the dark corridor, his footsteps swallowed by the carpet. Then he heard it.</em>\n<em>A sound — small, wet, somewhere behind him.</em>\n<em>He turned.</em>\n<em>Nothing.</em>\n<em>(Note: simple + complex + minor sentences used for pace.)</em>\n<strong>P5:</strong> 1. boys' and girls' (two separate teams)  2. It's raining … the city's slowest  3. dogs' bones … neighbour's garden</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 03 answers</h2>\n<p><strong>P1 — samples:</strong> 1. snaked around the terminal / was endless  2. petrified/terrified  3. succulent/fragrant/irresistible  4. strode/marched/glided  5. brutal/merciless/punishing\n<strong>P2 — samples:</strong> 1. The rain came down like a curtain being drawn.  2. His stomach performed a slow, unwelcome somersault.  3. The classroom buzzed like a hive about to swarm.  4. The day opened its arms to him.\n<strong>P3 — samples:</strong> 1. She bounced on the balls of her feet, holiday brochures fanned across the table.  2. His teacup sat unwashed for days, and he refilled it as if someone might arrive.  3. Heat climbed my neck, and I said nothing. Nothing at all.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 04 answers (Cheung Chau)</h2>\n<p><strong>1 (2):</strong> a whistle blows and climbers race up the tower of buns.\n<strong>2 (3):</strong> students, chefs, delivery riders; unpaid, not famous; locals of the island.\n<strong>3 (3):</strong> they are ordinary people with ordinary jobs; the contrast with their \"hero\" status for three minutes makes them relatable; the writer admires them.\n<strong>4 (3):</strong> in 1978 the tower collapsed; forty people were hurt; the race was banned for years.\n<strong>5 (4):</strong> \"even better\" does not mean safer <em>instead</em> of exciting; the risk is now shared and understood rather than hidden; the community values control and honesty over reckless danger.\n<strong>6 (5):</strong> e.g. \"an island that knows every one of their names\" — shows the community is small and close; \"the village decided\" — the whole community chooses together; \"the risk is now shared\" — collective responsibility.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 05 — Escalator summary model (~78 words)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>The escalator affected people very differently. For residents it was a lifeline, allowing them to commute up the steep hill without taxis or effort. For shop owners below the first stop, however, it was ruinous: customers passed overhead and never reached their doorsteps. Meanwhile, the city gained a tourist attraction that visitors queued to ride. For one young couple, it brought unexpected luck, since it made their halfway-up noodle shop affordable.</em>\n<em>(No opinion · linked · within limit · paraphrased.)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 06 — Model directed writing (ESL email, ~170 words)</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Hi Jay,</p>\n<p>Hope you're well! I'm writing because our school is running a charity fun run next month and I really think you should do it with me.</p>\n<p>It's a 5 km run around the reservoir on a Sunday morning, and every finisher raises money for a children's hospital. The route is flat and the scenery is genuinely beautiful, so it's not as hard as it sounds.</p>\n<p>I think it'll be brilliant because loads of our year are going, the music at the finish is always great, and there's a free barbecue afterwards. Honestly, even the training runs have been fun.</p>\n<p>All you need to bring is your trainers and a water bottle — I'll bring the sunscreen for both of us. Let me know by Friday so I can put your name down.</p>\n<p>Take care,\nMing</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>(Register consistent · all three bullets developed with opinion/detail · warm close.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>0500 letter to the editor — skeleton model:</strong> formal salutation; opening stating the issue and position; para 1 — demand outranks supply, teenagers have nowhere to play; para 2 — cost to health and community; para 3 — counter: \"some will say money is better spent elsewhere\" answered with long-term savings; close with a direct call to the council. <strong>Register must stay formal throughout.</strong></p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 07 — Band descriptors (write 350–450 words)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Content/ideas</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Vocab/sentences</th>\n<th>Accuracy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Top (9–10)</strong></td>\n<td>vivid, original, fully developed; clear controlling idea or arc</td>\n<td>deliberate camera movement or narrative shape; strong opening &amp; earned ending</td>\n<td>precise, varied; figurative language fresh, not clichéd</td>\n<td>minor slips only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Good (7–8)</strong></td>\n<td>relevant, engaging, mostly developed</td>\n<td>clear shape; some pace control</td>\n<td>some range; occasional weak word</td>\n<td>occasional tense/agreement slips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Developing (5–6)</strong></td>\n<td>relevant but thin; some clichés</td>\n<td>basic beginning/middle/end</td>\n<td>simple vocabulary; repetitive sentences</td>\n<td>regular errors but meaning clear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Foundation (3–4)</strong></td>\n<td>ideas listed, little development</td>\n<td>weak or absent plan</td>\n<td>limited vocabulary; run-ons</td>\n<td>frequent errors that sometimes obscure meaning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Below (1–2)</strong></td>\n<td>off-task or very short (&lt;150 words)</td>\n<td>none</td>\n<td>very basic</td>\n<td>errors dominate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 08 answers (R4)</h2>\n<p><strong>1 (3):</strong> \"tower of buns\" makes the buns sound stacked, almost architectural; it turns food into a structure to be climbed; the reader pictures something precarious and strange.\n<strong>2 (3):</strong> \"throw themselves\" suggests recklessness and total commitment; it shows they do it for excitement rather than reward; it makes the climb feel desperate or fearless.\n<strong>3 (4):</strong> the three-part list slows the sentence and feels deliberate and calm; each word answers the disaster (\"small\" against the collapse, \"careful\" against recklessness); \"wrapped in safety nets\" makes the danger contained; the effect is that the race feels mature rather than reckless.\n<strong>4 (5):</strong> the tone is wry and conversational (\"when you think about it\"); the aside feels like the writer nudging the reader to agree; it quietly states the theme — generosity, not danger, is the point; the short final clause ends the piece on a calm, confident note.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Module 09 — Mock paper answers</h2>\n<p><strong>Reading Q1 (2):</strong> Kwun Tong wet market, around seven in the morning.\n<strong>Q2 (3):</strong> has run the same stall 31 years; knows prices and almost everyone's name; emotional about her neighbours.\n<strong>Q3 (4):</strong> \"ribbons\" suggests the water is smooth and flowing; \"dark\" makes it look like ink or blood, hinting at loss; the everyday image is made slightly ominous.\n<strong>Q4 (5):</strong> she feels grief and loss; \"she stood in the empty stalls… and cried\"; the loss is of \"habits\" not buildings; the right to \"know a person by how they buy fish\" has gone.\n<strong>Q5 (6):</strong> short sentence lands like a period full stop after a long reflection; it states the true cost — invisibility, not just demolition; \"anyone\" is absolute, so the loss sounds total; ends the text on quiet devastation.\n<strong>Q6 summary (~92 words):</strong> The garden transformed the estate. Children who had stayed indoors grew vegetables and organised watering rotas. Elderly people, previously isolated, now had reason to leave their homes. The area's improved reputation raised rents on nearby blocks. However, some long-term tenants feared this would force out the very residents the garden had brought together. <em>(Effects, both positive and negative, paraphrased, within limit.)</em>\n<strong>Q7/Q8:</strong> mark with band descriptors; look for use of <strong>both</strong> texts and a clear stance.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Progress sheet</h2>\n<p>Fill <code>progress.md</code> after each session. Track the trend, not the single score.</p>"},{"id":"progress","label":"Progress Sheet","title":"Progress Sheet","group":"Reference","groupEmoji":"🗂️","emoji":"📊","blurb":"Your score sheet and Top 5 Errors list.","html":"<p>Update after every session. One line per week. <strong>Look at the trend, not one bad day.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Grammar /5</th>\n<th>Word choice /5</th>\n<th>Reading /5</th>\n<th>Summary /5</th>\n<th>Writing /5</th>\n<th>Notes / Top error</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 (diagnostic: ___ /70, band ___)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8 (mock: Paper1 ___/40, Paper2 ___/40)</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>My Top 5 Errors (keep this current — re-read before every piece of writing)</h2>\n<p>1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n5.</p>\n<h2>Wins this month</h2>\n<p>-</p>"}]};
